body:
The current processing model 8.1.4.2 executes the event loop by performance a macro-task, then micro-task checkpoint, then rendering loop update.

Since callbacks, such as timeouts and intervals, which are often scheduled or clamped to similar times, with the expectation of executing together are part of the macro-task queue. This can lead to updating the rendering of the document to a state which is unexpected by the developer.

While, there is no hard reason that the developer expectation should be that we provide any guarantees, as browsers, on the execution of timers with respect to one another, it turns out that the have a fairly strong promise today that nearby timers will be executed together. For instance, 2 100ms timers would be executed together without a render in between. However, since each is a task, it is possible that the rendering is ready to run after the first callback completes. This is made more complicated by late timer firing. Where say, 15 or so timers are "ready" at the time we start executing the first one. Generally browsers will try to catch up (I think, we at least do this in Edge) and allow timers a slightly higher priority than the rendering itself (technically we treat execution of timers as a loop in and of itself and we don't exit the loop until we think we are sufficiently caught up).

The rendering loop update may not be able to run between every type of task source.

If this spec were "implemented to its letter" then the following test case would print timeout 1, raf, timeout 2, but in every browser tested it prints 1, 2, raf most of the time, except for in Chrome which sometimes does indeed fire the RAF between the two timeouts, which surprised me and was a strange race condition.

More debugging in the fiddle, but a copy is attached below as well
https://jsfiddle.net/pscv9eou/

``` javascript
setTimeout(() => {
    console.log('timeout 1');
    requestAnimationFrame(() => { console.log('raf')});
  var now = performance.now();
  while (performance.now() - now < 20) {}
}, 100);
setTimeout(() => {
    console.log('timeout 2');
}, 100);
```
